//                  slot, a hand-on, a hold) is marked a judge heat's (server/jobs.ts sendJudgeHeatJob: its priority,
//                  the payload {phase_id} as ever, isJudgeHeatJob) and takes no
//                  place in the sandbox pool (takesNoSandboxPlace); an unmarked job gives its place back before the
//                  first call, and a marked job that finds a timing heat sends the phase's job again unmarked
//                  (codeHeatJobId `placed:<job id>`) and times nothing. Its calls go to the house judge through masq, or straight to
//                  superbot-router for a model it serves while MAKEFUN_SUPERBOT_ROUTER_URL and _KEY are set
//                  (grader/judge-call.ts openJudgeHeat; JudgeWorkerDeps). judgeCodeHeat reads the field's artifacts
//                  (each entry's final grade's output file judge.artifact, program.ts readOutputFile) and the house
//                  anchors from the judge directory, held to judge_sha256 (treeSha256). An entry that passed and
//                  wrote none, or one the judge cannot read (an image of no type masq takes), is left out of the
//                  field, and the log says why: its
//                  run is linked to the heat's record with no verdict, so it ranks below every judged entry and holds
//                  up nothing (a field with no entry left is a PermanentJobError: the scorer's gate is to fail them).
//                  It plans the schedule from the phase's plan seed (judgeHeatSetup), and opens the heat on a key of
//                  its own (judge/final/<n>, n the phase's next judge key: the header's `judge keys`) with no dollar
//                  cap: whatever its worst case, it opens and sends every round its schedule plans, with no budget
//                  weighed before any of them (judge-call.ts openJudgeHeat, its key minted uncapped); its request ledger
//                  (the calls still to come and the one fault re-send, each JUDGE_CALL_ATTEMPTS requests), its
//                  max_output_tokens, the one pinned model and the key's under-24 h life bound it. Only an unpriced
//                  model or a model blind to an image artifact holds it: the job is sent again
//                  JUDGE_HEAT_HOLD_RECHECK_MS on and pages once an hour (holdJudgeHeat; judgeHoldPage). A partial an
//                  earlier release kept (a heat it held before a round) resumes from its calls to the end.
//                  The calls the schedule has already planned (a batch: a Swiss round's, the
//                  playoff's) go out up to the heat's share of the judge pool at once (below; sendJudgeHeatCalls; the first alone while the
//                  heat may yet learn its model takes no forced tool and the process has not seen that model answer a
//                  forced form in the last 6 h: JudgeWorkerDeps.formsSeen), and each is taken strictly in the planned
//                  order, one that settles early held until those before it have: the calls, the record and the
//                  partial are a serial run's, byte for byte, and a heat that stops waits for every send in flight
//                  (each one metered) and keeps only the settled prefix. A call still out near its batch's end is
//                  backed up on a masq heat; a router heat sends each call once, bounded by the router itself
//                  (JudgeWorkerDeps.backup, default grader/judge-backup.ts judgeBackupRuleOf: none on a router key,
//                  else JUDGE_BACKUP: the batch's last 20%, min 2, once its newest send is older than max(8 s,
//                  1.25 x the median answer), or any send 90 s old; 3 sends a call at most, backups at most a quarter of the primaries, and only out of requests
//                  the heat's ledger can spare beside every call still to send and its fault re-send, so the ledger
//                  bounds it as before). The first send to answer with a verdict decides the
//                  call; its other sends are aborted at once and kept as the record's `spares` (judge-heat.ts), never
//                  scored; a call faults only when every send of it faulted. JUDGE_HEAT_CONCURRENCY (64) is a heat's
//                  width, what a live ramp (2026-11-11) found the shared model pool gives without slowing every call;
//                  the platform's budget is wider, JUDGE_POOL_BUDGET (96: in flight together it shed nothing and ran
//                  each call at 79.6% speed, about a fifth more work in total): every live heat, a final's and a dev
//                  grade's provisional one alike, on every grader process, shares one budget of JUDGE_POOL_BUDGET
//                  judge sends in flight, backups counted, and no heat holds more than its own width. A heat takes a
//                  seat in it once its key is minted (openJudgePoolSeat: a file `<id>.json` under the blob store's judge-pool directory, which
//                  every process reads, holding what the heat could use right now and when it was written, and beside
//                  it `<id>.seat` (makefun/judge-pool/v2), which an old process never reads, adding the heat's class and
//                  its sends in flight; rewritten when those move, at most each JUDGE_POOL_REFRESH_MS, and at least
//                  each JUDGE_POOL_BEAT_MS; removed when the send ends, however it ends) and keeps its sends in flight
//                  under its share (judgePoolShares: max-min fair over the seats' wants, every seat at least 4 of what
//                  it wants; a heat alone keeps its 64, two at once get 48 each, and one whose round is in its tail
//                  gives the rest to the other; final first: while a final's heat is live, the finals take their
//                  shares first and the dev grades' provisional heats split what is left, at least 1 each; contender
//                  last: a dev contender round's seat, `<id>.seat` alone, splits only what every other seat's share
//                  leaves, from 0, its sender still keeping one send in flight). A heat
//                  that joins, or whose want rises, takes its share less what the others hold in flight past theirs
//                  (judgePoolAllowance: no send in flight is cancelled when a share falls, so without it a final
//                  joining three full practice heats put 136 in flight), growing as their sends complete, so the
//                  pool stays within the budget plus the floors. A seat not written for JUDGE_POOL_STALE_MS (its grader died) counts no more, so its
//                  share goes back to the others within 30 s; the pool's own I/O failing leaves the heat bounded by
//                  its own width alone, as before the pool (JudgeWorkerDeps.pool: by default only on the environment's
//                  broker). The pool's pushback narrows the width under the share: each rate-limited re-send a turn
//                  finds (judge-call.ts JudgeHeat.pushbacks) halves the heat's width in force (never under 16), holds
//                  its backups 30 s, and each quiet 15 s gives 8 back (JUDGE_PUSHBACK_FLOOR), no call in flight
//                  cancelled. Each call is kept as it settles (the
//                  response blob first, then the calls and their spares: judgeHeatPartialPath, under
//                  judgeHeatResumeKey), with what it spent and the key held open, so an attempt that stops goes on
//                  from the next call, its open key retired first against what it metered. The partial goes to disk
//                  at most every JUDGE_HEAT_SAVE_EVERY_MS or JUDGE_HEAT_SAVE_EVERY_CALLS calls (sendJudgeHeat's
//                  throttle, which a final's heat sets: its saveEvery), and always at a re-send, a stop, an abort and the end: written once a call, a
//                  16k-call heat's whole partial (25 MB, canonical) held the event loop 0.3-1 s a call, past Node's
//                  250 ms connect attempt timer, and timed out every connect on its pod (2026-11-13). A process
//                  killed outright loses only the calls since the last save, which the next attempt judges again.
//                  A screened final's screen, fixed before its first call, is kept apart in a sidecar
//                  (judgeHeatPartialStore, judgeHeatScreenPath), written once, not with every save.
//                  A refusal the router names a wait for is no fault: the call waits it out whole and sends again, as
//                  often as the router asks, while the job's signal stands (judge-call.ts, the header's `router`).
//                  A call that faults is sent once again (on superbot-router, only for a fault the router never saw);
//                  a second fault off superbot-router, or more calls with no verdict than
//                  judge.invalid_share_max allows (stopped as soon as no call to come can mend it), fails the heat: a
//                  PermanentJobError with a JudgeHeatError its cause, nothing written, the dead job paged
//                  (codeHeatDeadPage). A fault the router answered finally (judge-call.ts JudgeCallFault.final: no
//                  layer sends it again), or on superbot-router a second fault the router never saw (a refused
//                  connect, the Gateway's own 503 or 403), ends the attempt at once, nothing more sent once the calls in flight settle,
//                  its calls and spares kept and its key retired, and throws a plain Error: the queue retries the job
//                  (code-heat's retryDelay 300 s, backoff), which resumes from the calls kept; on the job's last
//                  attempt it fails the heat as above, saying the queue's attempts are spent. An attempt that kept a
//                  call it did not start with spends no attempt on such a fault: it hands the heat on to the job of
//                  the next CODE_HEAT_RECHECK_MS slot (codeHeatJobId `wait:<slot>`, handOnJudgeHeat), on its last
//                  attempt too, its live document left, and completes. The heat done, its
//                  key is retired (masq's spend beside the meter: flags logged), the record stored, and each field entry's run written its score built again with its
//                  member's verdict (scoreWithJudge: performance.judge) and run.judge_heat_sha256, in one transaction
//                  held to the result each run had (a run written since retries the job, from the calls kept). A
//                  regrade rebuilds the verdict from that record (grader/regrade.ts judgeVerdictOf), never by calling
//                  a model. A final's CPU work runs off this thread (grader/judge-heat-tasks.ts on server/offthread):
//                  its schedule planned again once a batch (sendJudgeHeatCalls' default, judgeHeatScheduleFor), and
//                  at its end its record built, held once, digested and scored (judgeHeatRecordEnd, sent the calls'
//                  texts the partial writer keeps); a resumed attempt reads its kept partial back once and holds
//                  its calls to the heat (keptJudgeHeat: judgeHeatKeptReadOffThread, judgeHeatKeptCheckOffThread),
//                  parsing back here only the calls it resumes from, in slices; a regrade reads a record back held
//                  once a process (regrade.ts loadHeldJudgeHeatRecord)
//   provisional judge
//                  a judged competition's dev grade that passed its gate plays its artifact against every house
//                  anchor in both orders before it is recorded (withProvisionalJudge; judge-heat.ts's provisional
//                  stage, judgeProvisionalCalls: two calls an anchor; sent as the judge heat's are, up to its share
//                  of the judge pool at once (a seat of its own, as a final's heat takes) and kept in planned order, a
//                  batch's last calls backed up by the
//                  same rule and their other sends kept in memory for the record's spares), on a key of its own
//                  (judge/dev/<n>) minted with no dollar cap: no spend is read, weighed or waited on (the header's
//                  `judge keys`). Its record is stored and linked as run.judge_heat_sha256, and its
//                  score carries the verdict (performance.judge, provisional: its win rate against the anchors),
//                  shown on the dev board and never weighed. A grade the heat cannot judge is recorded unjudged and
//                  says so in the log (no gateway, a judge model with no price, no artifact or one the judge
//                  cannot read, a second faulted call, a call the router answered finally (never sent again: the
//                  heat stops at once), more calls with no verdict than judge.invalid_share_max
//                  allows); nothing is sent again, and nothing paged. A regrade rebuilds the verdict from the record.
//                  A bundle naming a ladder (judge.ladder: grader/judge-ladder-tree.ts readJudgeLadder, beside the judge
//                  directory) plays a v3 heat on it instead: the anchors once each, then its adaptive rounds against
//                  the ladder's members of fixed strength (judge-heat.ts judgeHeatPlannedCalls), rated against them;
//                  every grade plays every adaptive round of the ladder's design
//   provisional reuse
//                  a dev artifact byte for byte one an earlier entry of the phase was judged on (server/entries/
//                  screen/artifact.ts judgedSameArtifact, oldest first) takes that verdict with no judge call: the
//                  earlier record's key the current judge's (provisionalRecordJudgeKey, provisionalJudgeKey), its
//                  phase seed the heat's, and its verdict on the artifact one the regrade rebuilds (grader/regrade.ts
//                  judgeRecordVerdictOf: a provisional field's one entrant member on the same bytes). The run links
//                  that record; no key is claimed, nothing reserved or spent. A miss, or a fault in the lookup (logged
//                  with its stack), is judged as above. Either way, once the heat ends, the artifact is written to the
//                  phase's entry_artifact (its fingerprint, record, whether it was reused, and near_of; no record when
//                  the heat judged nothing), a fault there logged and the heat kept (recordProvisionalArtifact). No row
//                  is written for a heat that ended on the platform's fault or an unpriced model, nor for one that never
//                  reached an artifact the judge reads (no judge tree or ladder, no artifact, one the judge cannot
//                  read). Two identical artifacts judged at the same moment may both pay
//   provisional rejudge
//                  an admin's POST /api/admin/competitions/:slug/rejudge-provisional (server/routes/admin/grading.ts),
//                  and every 15 minutes the ops process's provisional-rejudge-sweep for each judged competition still
//                  taking entries (server/grading/provisional-rejudge-sweep.ts), sends, for each kept dev entry whose latest grade run passed its gate and is not yet judged on the
//                  bundle's current judge, a grade-dev job naming that run (server/jobs.ts GradeJob `rejudge`, an
//                  L1-shaped job: kind regrade_l1, reproduces_run_id the run). Its handler (rejudgeProvisional) runs
//                  no program and records no run: it plays the run's artifact in a provisional heat on the current
//                  judge (provisionalJudgeHeat, the dev grade's own: anchors, judge directory, house text, its uncapped
//                  key, the record stored first) under the seed the run recorded, and writes the run its score built
//                  again with the new verdict and the link to the new record, in one transaction held to the result
//                  it had (GRADE_CHANGE told), as a new dev grade would record it; a heat that judges nothing records
//                  the run unjudged (old verdict and link dropped), as a dev grade is, but never over the platform's
//                  fault or a judge model with no price: a faulted heat writes nothing and throws for the job to retry,
//                  an unpriced one writes nothing and is sent again on a later UTC day, the verdict kept. A run recorded
//                  unjudged (an earlier release's daily cap among the reasons) holds no verdict, so it is sent as any
//                  run not judged on the current judge is. What a verdict was judged on is
//                  its record's ProvisionalJudgeOn (format, agent, judge_sha256, media, artifact, invalid share, house
//                  text, and on a ladder its digest, rounds, games and spread), keyed by provisionalJudgeKeyOf: a run
//                  whose record's key is the current judge's (provisionalJudgeKey) is judged already, and is skipped,
//                  by the route and again by the job (a record that played fewer of the ladder's rounds, as an earlier
//                  release's daily cap cut some short, counts as judged on it: provisionalRecordJudgeKey given the
//                  judge). A ladder landing on a bundle moves its key, so each kept dev entry is judged again on it. A
//                  final phase's run is refused: its verdict is its heat's. Nothing is published after it
//   dev scrutiny  a screened bundle's dev heat (judge.screened.dev_rounds above 0, on a ladder) records its money-place
//                  scrutiny at the phase's dev line, read as the heat sets up (server/grading/dev-scrutiny-line.ts:
//                  the K-th best dev interval low, K the last paid place; no line stored, or none while fewer than K
//                  entrants are rated, records none): an entry whose interval high over its ladder rounds reaches the
//                  line plays dev_rounds more (grader/judge-heat.ts JudgeHeatLadder.scrutiny), so its call count is a
//                  bound (judgeHeatCallsBounded). A rejudge job carrying `scrutiny_top_up` {judge_heat_sha256} (sent
//                  by the sweep once a record a stored line, and none while one of it waits: server/grading/
//                  provisional-rejudge-sweep.ts devScrutinyTopUpJobId) tops a run on the current judge up
//                  instead of skipping it as current (scrutinyTopUp): its record's setup scrutinised at the phase's line as
//                  stored when the job runs (an older release's job: the `line_micro` it carries), its calls and
//                  spares kept verbatim (never asked again), only the scrutiny rounds sent, and every grade run and
//                  entry_artifact row of the phase that linked the old record relinked to the new one; a run not on
//                  the current judge is judged afresh at that line. A baseline's or a house entry's run (no prize
//                  place, so the line never counts it), a run that no longer links the job's record, and a phase with
//                  no line standing are topped up with nothing. A top-up never drops a verdict, and only its end on
//                  the platform's fault throws for the queue to retry: any other end writes nothing and is final for
//                  that job. One that played (the record extended into a new, scrutinised one) is never sent again;
//                  one that played nothing (the stored line had moved above the record's interval high by the time it
//                  ran) leaves the record unscrutinised and linked, and the sweep sends it again once the line is
//                  stored anew and reaches it
//   contender     a screened bundle's sealed dev contender round (grader/judge-contender.ts; playContenderRound,
//                  handed the chain's fold, the round's setup and calls and its players' artifacts) is played as a
//                  provisional heat is, on the bundle's one named model alone (never a pool, the switch or a
//                  planner's pick): its judge directory and ladder read and the setup held to them and to the chain,
//                  judgePreflight, a key claimed as stage 'provisional' in the dev phase's keys, its sends through a
//                  'contender' seat (only what every other seat's share leaves), each call kept as it settles in the
//                  phase's partial (judgeContenderPartialPath, under judgeContenderResumeKey) so a retry asks only the
//                  calls still to come, the key retired whatever happens, its record stored as a blob before it is
//                  returned. It says counts only, every sha256 masked: the record is sealed until the deadline
//   judge keys    every key a phase's judge heats mint is kept as a file of its own under the blob store
//                  (judgeKeysDir: JudgeKeyFile, `<attempt>.json`, claimed by a link that fails on a name taken, so
//                  no two heats share a number), with what its heat metered and what masq said it spent once retired.
//                  No heat weighs a spend against it: a provisional heat claims its number and mints its key with no
//                  dollar cap, reading no other key; a final heat keeps its keys for their numbers and what they spent,
//                  a failed heat's on any host among them (a key another attempt left open retired first). A claim
//                  that never minted is removed by the first read past JUDGE_KEY_CLAIM_EXPIRE_MS (10 min) after its
//                  claim: its heat stopped between claim and mint (readJudgeKeys). A file an earlier release wrote may
//                  hold a provisional heat's worst case in reserved_micro_usd; nothing reads it
//   scratch        a grade's executions and scorer, and a regrade's re-execution and scorer, work in
//                  directories under the work root (GradeWorkerDeps.workRoot). Handed its job's context
//                  (GradeJobContext), the handler names them for the job: its owner is regradeOwner of
//                  the job's id, `regrade-<job id>`, the same on each attempt and no other job's, so
//                  they are `exec-<owner>-XXXXXX` and `score-<owner>-XXXXXX` (grade.ts
//                  GradeRequest.owner, regrade.ts RegradeL1Request.owner). Each attempt first
//                  removes what an attempt of the job that died left so named (grade.ts
//                  reapGradeScratch), each path logged (scratchReapLine). What no attempt comes back
//                  for is removed by the workers' start (server/pipeline/index.ts sweepDeadWork), and
//                  only once all three hold (removeIfDead): no active job on the start's database
//                  holds its owner, no one has touched it for six hours (its newest change, its own or
//                  a direct entry's, older than grader/sandbox/network.ts STALE_AFTER_MS), and no live
//                  process holds it open or as its cwd (processesHolding). One younger, held or not to
//                  be asked about is kept and logged: a job on another database sharing the work root,
//                  or a run handed no job, may still work in it. Handed no context (a test, a direct
//                  call), it names them for no one, `exec-XXXXXX` and `score-XXXXXX`, as before;
//                  nothing removes those after a crash. An agent/v1 regrade's is its own (`regrade`)
//   layout         the k8s sandbox runs an entry's programs on one of two layouts (grader/sandbox/layout.ts): `split`,
//                  today's (an agent/v1 trial's entry, env and forwarders each a pod, a heat's entry and gateway two, a
//                  program run one, its /output meter a `watch` container in it), or `pod`, one sandbox pod an entry, its forwarders and gateway lanes of
//                  the grader's lanes pod (server/sandbox-host/services.ts). Only agent/v1 and scrape/v1 contests take
//                  `pod` (layout.ts POD_LAYOUT_KINDS). A program/v1 grade is one pod on `split` and never takes `pod`
//                  (the owner, 2026-11-12: "always 1 pod max per entry for all future competitions pls"), so the
//                  one-execution grade (a replay, patch or program/v1 grade and its regrades), its timing and the
//                  code heat read no layout and run on the grader's own sandbox, as before the layout existed. What
//                  decides for an agent/v1 run or a scrape heat (OnePodDeps, the pipeline's):
//                    - dormant: go-live's instant (`from`, the one-pod-from site setting) is read ONCE as the process
//                      boots; absent then, every job this process starts runs `split` for its whole life, exactly as
//                      before the layout existed: no flag, no one_pod payload, no extra read, no lanes made
//                    - a fresh job (an agent/v1 grade's run, a scrape heat) takes sandboxLayoutOf(slug, its harness
//                      kind, the competition row's created_at, from) (layoutOfFresh). On `pod` an agent/v1 run is
//                      flagged ONE_POD_FLAG as it starts and every grade-trial job of it says `one_pod: true`
//                      (server/jobs.ts counts it one pod, in its own slots); a heat's runs are flagged as they are
//                      recorded (run.flags: beside the score, never in it, regrade.ts RUN_ONLY_FLAGS). A process that
//                      cannot host `pod` (no k8s sandbox with a pod of its own: the ops worker, a Job) runs such a job
//                      `split`, and says so once a contest in its log
//                    - a run's later jobs follow the run's flag, never the table: its trials (run.flags, read with the
//                      run; the job's `one_pod` carried on every re-send), an agent/v1 L2 regrade, and a retried grade
//                      job whose run a dead attempt made, in every process, dormant or not (its flags read with its
//                      trials, in the one read its retry made of them: trialsWithFlags; no read more). A hindsight
//                      audit and a dev stress (grader/dev-stress.ts) still read their run's flag, which no run of their
//                      kind (pumpfun-replay/v2) carries. Hosting is
//                      not gated on `from`: any process that can host runs a flagged run's job on `pod` (the lanes
//                      client made on its first such job); one that cannot fails the job as the platform's fault
//                      (PlatformFaultError, one plain sentence: onePodHostOf), never re-running it on `split`, whose
//                      CPU and memory room differ
//                  On `pod`: the one-pod view of the sandbox (K8sSandbox.onePod) for a scrape heat, whose trials take
//                  the lanes' `hosted` and `door` besides; an agent/v1 pass takes the view, `hosted`, `door`, the contest's own
//                  podRequestsOf and its entry's lane key for the trial (grader/agent.ts podEntryLaneKey of the
//                  fail-key secret, the trial row's run and the trial: onePodEntryKey, the same on its live pass and
//                  its in-job replay; the key alone leaves this file, never the secret; the run's trials manifest
//                  carries it once the run is recorded, AgentTrialRecord.entry_key, where an L2 reads it), and
//                  needs room for trialPods(harness, 'pod') (1) before it sets out. The lanes pod is
//                  opened (OnePodLanes.open) before a `pod` pass or heat sets out; no room for it (a NodeRoomError) hands
//                  the trial back as the node's (`node room`), no pass spent and no key minted. A trial's scorer stays
//                  a pod of its own (one pod of room), made after the trial's pod is deleted (grader/agent.ts)
//
// practice windows  a dev phase whose bundle carries practice_windows (contracts/competition.ts Phase.practice_windows:
//                  {count, hours}, a pumpfun-replay/v2 harness; its dev dataset is null, each window's attached to a
//                  practice_window row) grades the entry on every window, gradeEras:
//                    - the run row first, `running`, under agentRunId of the grade job (so a retried job finds it)
//                      with no dataset_sha256 or seed (the median window is not known yet)
//                    - each window in key order (practiceWindowKeys): one the run's run_era rows hold finished (its
//                      dataset, seed and Score as the window has them) is kept, so a retry resumes; any other is
//                      graded on eraWindowPlan (its own dataset, the seed phaseSeed(competition key, 'dev', that
//                      dataset's sha256) and the public fail key of it, no final window), two executions byte
//                      equal (grade.ts gradeEntry), and its run_era row and blobs recorded (record.ts recordEra)
//                    - the grade stops at the first window not ranked (failed, timed out, two executions that
//                      disagree, an unmetered cost): that window's outcome is the run's, its own Score the result
//                      (the first such window by key is what score.ts aggregateEras returns)
//                    - otherwise the run's result is aggregateEras over every window's Score (regrade.ts erasResult),
//                      and the run's dataset_sha256, seed, fail key, action log, trace and stderr are the median
//                      window's (the result's performance.eras.rank_window); exec_count and wall_ms are the sums over
//                      the windows, flags the median Score's
//                  A regrade of a run with run_era rows follows them window by window (regrade.ts regradeErasL1,
//                  regradeErasL2): each row's dataset resolved by its unique sha256, its own seed and public fail key,
//                  the regrade's run a run_era row of its own for each. A dev phase without practice_windows, and a
//                  run without run_era rows, take the path they always have.
//
// Harness faults (sandbox, scorer, a dataset the sim refuses) propagate so the job retries, and so
// does a committed fail key that cannot be derived yet (FailKeyUnavailableError, paged: the header's
// `fail key`); a job
// that can never succeed (no bundle, no snapshot, no dataset, a dataset that fails its hashes, a
// stored authorship summary that does not validate, nothing to regrade, an instance bundle whose
// instance does not check, an agent/v1 run its regrade cannot re-derive, a scrape/v1 competition with
// no house baseline, a scrape/v1 L2 regrade) is a PermanentJobError and goes to the dead-letter queue.
// An instance bundle with no instance drawn yet to grade on is the platform's (`not drawn` below).
//
// platform fault   a clmm score whose LVR (fees - costs - hedged PnL) parts from LVR leg by leg by
//                  more than rounding (sim/clmm/metrics.ts ClmmLvrMismatchError, which every clmm
//                  plan's score throws: harnesses.ts clmmPlan, assertClmmLvr) is a fault of the
//                  scorer or the engine, never the entry's, and no retry mends it: the grade or
//                  regrade throws server/jobs.ts PlatformFaultError (a PermanentJobError naming the
//                  entry or run, the phase and both LVRs), so the job dead-letters at once and is
//                  paged as a `dead grade job` (below), and no run is recorded, so nothing is posted
//                  and the entry is never scored on it; the pipeline's grade stage puts the entry
//                  back as it was rather than failing it (server/pipeline/index.ts). The scorer is
//                  GradeWorkerDeps.clmmScorer (default scoreClmmActionLog); the check holds whatever
//                  it is
//
// not drawn        an instance-drawn phase whose instance is not drawn yet is the platform's, never
//                  the entry's: no instance.yaml in the competition directory at all (nothing drawn:
//                  the practice draw writes it), on the final phase no scored draw record yet (no
//                  instance-draw-0.json: the scored round is not drawn), or an instance tree that is
//                  not there at all (the practice instance or trial not drawn yet: `instance`,
//                  `agent/v1` above; a scored one whose trees are gone). Each is an
//                  InstanceNotDrawnError, a PlatformFaultError naming what is not there and the draw
//                  that mends it. No run is recorded. The pipeline's grade stage puts the entry back as
//                  it was and hands the grade job on until the draw is there (server/pipeline/index.ts),
//                  so a grade's is never paged as a `dead grade job` (below) by its handler (one a stage
//                  with no queue could not hand on dead-letters, and the sweep pages it); a wait past
//                  a day (a draw never made, or a competition directory or MAKEFUN_SEALED_DIR that is
//                  not where the draw wrote) pages the admins once, by the pipeline, and the entry
//                  waits on (server/pipeline/index.ts INSTANCE_WAIT_PAGE_MS); a regrade's
//                  dead-letters and is paged as any PlatformFaultError. A practice tree (the dev
//                  phase's instance or trial) that is there but is not instance.yaml's practice draw
//                  (it digests otherwise, its instance digest is another, or it holds what a sealed
//                  tree may not: a draw of an older instance.yaml, or damaged) is an
//                  InstanceNotDrawnError too: the release's practice draw re-draws it in place
//                  (server/competitions/practice-draw.ts drawCommittedPractice), so its dev grades
//                  wait for it. So does an agent/v1 dev phase whose instance.yaml was drawn by another
//                  generator than the stored bundle's (a release between its deploy and its seed:
//                  `re-drawn practice` above). What else is there but does not check (an instance.yaml that cannot
//                  be read or does not check, a draw record chain that does not verify, a scored
//                  tree that is not its record) stays a PermanentJobError
//
// ranked floor     a clmm phase's window is held to its phase's floor of whole blocks of the
//                  harness's interval.block_hours (contracts/clmm-protocol.ts clmmPhaseBlocksIssue):
//                  CLMM_RANKED_MIN_BLOCKS (28) on the final phase, whose board pays the prizes,
//                  CLMM_MIN_BLOCKS (8) on dev; under it the grade or regrade is a PermanentJobError
//                  (harnesses.ts ClmmShortWindowError, its reason naming the phase, the blocks and
//                  the floor)
//
// dead grade job   a grade job that dies for good pages the admins, once per job and attempt: its
//                  GRADE_JOB_DEAD_ACTION row (the system's, subject the submission), written under
//                  the submission row's lock before the line goes out, is what keeps it from being
//                  paged again. Found in two places. The job's own handler as it dies, when it is
//                  handed its job context (GradeJobContext, which the pipeline's grade stage hands
//                  on: a PermanentJobError, or any throw on its last attempt, isLastAttempt). And a
//                  sweep (createDeadGradeJobPager), run by every grade job before it grades and by
//                  the forfeit sweep's job every 15 minutes (server/pipeline/index.ts startWorkers),
//                  over every grade job pg-boss holds failed that no row records: one that died
//                  outside its handler (refused by the grade stage before it, expired with its
//                  process gone, failed by hand) or whose handler was handed no context. The page
//                  (deps.page, default console.error: a "[grade] ALERT:" line) names the
//                  competition, the entry, the phase, the job, how it died and its last error, and
//                  the admin route that queues it again: a regrade's POST
//                  /api/admin/runs/<run>/regrade, a grade's POST
//                  /api/admin/submissions/<entry>/grade/resend with the dead job's id, which sends it
//                  again under a fresh job id (server/routes/admin/grading.ts registerGradeResend).
//                  A grade (kind grade) whose last attempt its grader's stop or kill cut (pg-boss's
//                  output for it: server/jobs.ts isRolloutCutOutput) is sent again that way by the
//                  pager itself when it holds the job queue, before its row is written (grading.ts
//                  `automatic resend`: the route's refusals, at most AUTO_GRADE_RESEND_CAP per entry
//                  and phase); its row is then unpaged (why_not, resent_as). Refused or capped, it
//                  pages as any death, saying why it was not sent again.
//                  The sweep also takes a scrape/v1 final's heat job (scrape-heat, not a re-judging)
//                  whose last attempt was so cut, when it holds the job queue: sent again by itself
//                  (grading.ts resendCutHeatJob, at most AUTO_GRADE_RESEND_CAP per phase), else paged
//                  in the "[scrape] ALERT:" words with why not; either way a GRADE_JOB_DEAD_ACTION row
//                  subject the phase (kind heat) decides it. A heat's other deaths its handler pages.
//                  A job held on its entrant's entry secrets (its error opening
//                  ENTRY_SECRETS_UNOPENED_HOLD_REASON: `entry secrets` above) is paged as held, never
//                  failed: the entry waits ungraded, and the fix is the seal private keys that open the
//                  secrets its error names (MAKEFUN_ENTRY_SECRET_SEAL_PRIVATE_KEYS_FILE; on the cluster,
//                  Secret makefun-entry-secret-seal), then the same route (heldGradeJobPage).
//                  An entry that is out (cancelled, or a refusal that stands: a duplicate snapshot or a
//                  committed secret, server/pipeline/publish.ts isRefused) was never to
//                  be graded: its death is recorded and logged, not paged. A job cancelled while it
//                  ran (a superseded entry's grade: its attempt's signal aborted and its pg-boss row
//                  `cancelled`, as server/jobs.ts counts its outcome) did not die: no row, no page,
//                  a log line. A row or a sweep that
//                  fails is logged and never fails the job (its own error stands). The row goes
//                  through payouts/audit.ts recordAudit, whose AuditAction names grade.job_dead.
//
// The hindsight-audit job handler (server/jobs.ts PipelineHandlers.hindsightAudit:
// createHindsightAuditHandler; its rules are server/routes/admin/hindsight.ts's, its audit
// grader/hindsight-audit.ts's):
//   sweep {phase_id}    on a drawn sealed window's final phase (its draw attached the phase's dataset:
//                       admin/hindsight.ts drawnFinal) whose grading has settled, one job per audit
//                       target lacking hindsight_clean and per appeal filed with no record yet
//                       (server/jobs.ts hindsightAuditJobId); anything else is a no-op
//   one entry           its latest final grade run (finished, a replay score) audited with auditEntry:
//                       the snapshot materialized as the grader mounts it; the drawn masked window
//                       (the phase's dataset row) as the real window, its digest the run's; the real
//                       run's hello seed and fail key (the run's, else derived again as grading
//                       derives it: phaseFailKey by the phase's scheme, a FailKeyUnavailableError
//                       retried and paged alike, the header's `fail key`) and its stored net PnL; the
//                       env-lane entry secrets the run recorded, re-opened (recordedEnvSecrets, the
//                       header's `entry secrets`: one erased since is a PermanentJobError, one this
//                       grader cannot open a retryable Error, nothing run either way); the
//                       static scan against the drawn source
//                       (the draw's source dataset row) with the dev phase's window as `background`
//                       (its public amounts identify nothing), the index built once per window pair
//                       and kept (buildHindsightIndex), and the entrant's entry wallets and payout
//                       wallet as ignoreAddresses; CHEAT_CHECK.m surrogates per rung (appeal_m on an
//                       appeal); the seeds from one master secret, HKDF-SHA256 of the server secret
//                       (deps.failKeySecret, on either fail-key scheme) over (kind, entry, run,
//                       appeal attempt) (hindsightSeedMaster), so a
//                       retried job rebuilds the same surrogates and an appeal gets fresh ones. The
//                       record must pass checkAuditRecord, then admin/hindsight.ts
//                       recordHindsightAudit keeps it (and, on an audit, records the check). An audit
//                       of a run already recorded, or an appeal with none awaiting its re-run, is a
//                       no-op; what can never succeed (no draw, no finished replay run, an AuditError)
//                       is a PermanentJobError. The audit works in `<work root>/hindsight-<owner>-XXXXXX`
//                       (HINDSIGHT_SCRATCH_PREFIX), named for its job (regradeOwner of the job's id,
//                       the same on each attempt). Before anything else, each attempt removes what an
//                       attempt of the job that died left so named (regrade.ts reapScratch), each path
//                       logged (scratchReapLine). What no attempt comes back for is removed by the
//                       workers' start (server/pipeline/index.ts sweepDeadWork), and only once all
//                       three hold (removeIfDead): no active job on the start's database holds its
//                       owner, no one has touched it for six hours (its newest change, its own or a
//                       direct entry's, older than grader/sandbox/network.ts STALE_AFTER_MS), and no
//                       live process holds it open or as its cwd (processesHolding). One younger, held
//                       or not to be asked about is kept and logged: a job on another database sharing
//                       the work root, or a run handed no job, may still work in it. While a scrape/v1
//                       heat holds this host's lease under the `host` lease scope (scrapeHostHeldBy:
//                       `scrape/v1` above; under `egress` nothing here waits), an audit
//                       runs nothing: once the no-op cases are ruled out it hands its job on to the next
//                       recheck slot (SCRAPE_HOST_RECHECK_MS, server/jobs.ts hindsightAuditWaitJobId),
//                       so no surrogate shares the CPU with a timed heat and none spends its expiry on it.
//                       An attempt stops once its signal aborts (JobContext.signal: pg-boss gives the
//                       attempt up at its expiry, and retries the job): its replays run in a sandbox
//                       bound to the signal (grade.ts stoppableSandbox), so the abort kills the replay
//                       running and no later one starts, and an attempt given up before its audit
//                       materializes nothing. Its scratch is removed as ever, nothing is recorded (not
//                       even a record its audit finished), and it rejects with the signal's reason,
//                       never a PermanentJobError: the retry audits afresh, never beside a first that
//                       goes on replaying, and records once.
//   dead                an entry job that dies for good pages the admins, once per job: its
//                       HINDSIGHT_AUDIT_DEAD_ACTION row (the system's, subject the submission), written
//                       under the submission row's lock before the line goes out, is what keeps a
//                       later sweep from paging the job again. Found in two places. The job's own
//                       handler as it dies (a PermanentJobError, or any throw on its last attempt:
//                       isLastAttempt of JobContext.retryLimit), when the audit's entry is an
//                       unchecked audit target on that run and no newer audit job of the run is held,
//                       or an appeal waits for that re-run. And each sweep, after its sends, for every
//                       unchecked target whose latest audit job pg-boss holds failed, and every
//                       appeal still waiting whose latest re-run job (kind appeal, of the run it
//                       re-runs) pg-boss holds failed (admin/hindsight.ts latestAuditJobOf): one that
//                       died outside its handler, expired with its process gone, or failed by hand.
//                       The page (deps.page, default console.error: a "[hindsight] ALERT:" line) names
//                       the competition, the entry, the run, the job, how it died and its error, and
//                       the fix. A dead audit's: a verdict at POST /api/admin/submissions/<id>/hindsight
//                       (deadAuditOf), given with no audit record, open until the final phase is
//                       finalized (at or after its ends_at too, which the page then says). A dead
//                       appeal re-run's: a verdict deciding the appeal without it, likewise open until
//                       finalize (deadAppealOf past ends_at); its handler pages it only while no newer
//                       re-run job of the appeal is held. Reading or writing a page that fails is
//                       logged, and never fails the job (its own error stands) or the sweep.
//
// The dev-stress job handler (server/jobs.ts PipelineHandlers.devStress) is grader/dev-stress.ts's
// createDevStressHandler, which reads this file's helpers (practiceWindowsOf, storedEraWindows, entryRuntime, regradeOwner,
// scratchReapLine, scrapeHostHeldBy) and which nothing here imports back: the eras dev grade's stress on the kit's shared
// block surrogates, its scratch `<work root>/devstress-<owner>-XXXXXX` (DEV_STRESS_SCRATCH_PREFIX), a scrape/v1 heat
// waited out as an audit's (server/jobs.ts devStressWaitJobId).

